      xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="https://apps.nextcloud.com/schema/apps/info.xsd">
  <id>grafana_sync</id>
  <name>Grafana Sync</name>
  <summary>Your Grafana dashboards as native, editable, backed-up Nextcloud files</summary>
  <description><![CDATA[
Grafana Sync surfaces your Grafana dashboards as native Nextcloud files. Map a
Grafana **folder** to a Nextcloud folder and every dashboard inside it shows up as
a `.grafana` file — with a real file icon, deep links back to Grafana, and a
per-folder sync mode.

- **Sync** — full two-way ownership. Edit a dashboard in the Files app, over
  WebDAV, or from your desktop client and the change pushes back to Grafana; a sync
  folder doubles as a complete, restorable backup of every dashboard in it.
- **Link** — a lightweight pointer (uid, title, URL) that opens the dashboard in
  Grafana. Ideal for operator/GitOps-provisioned dashboards that are owned
  elsewhere and should never be written back.

Because Grafana has real folders, the mapping is a plain folder-to-folder mirror —
no tagging scheme to maintain. The link between a file and its dashboard is the
stable Grafana **uid** stored in the file's metadata, not the filename, so
renaming, moving, and restoring never break the connection.

⚠️ Early development — this build ships the admin **connection** panel (point the
app at Grafana, store a service-account token, and test it) and the **folder
mapping** panel (bind a Grafana folder to a Nextcloud folder, with a mode and
format). The actual dashboard sync those mappings drive lands in later releases.

This is a community integration and is not affiliated with, endorsed by, or
sponsored by Grafana Labs. "Grafana" and the Grafana logo are trademarks of
Grafana Labs, used here only to identify the service this app integrates with.
  ]]></description>
  <version>0.1.0</version>
  <licence>agpl</licence>
  <author>Kelly Ferrone</author>
  <namespace>GrafanaSync</namespace>
  <category>integration</category>
  <category>files</category>
  <!--
    DECLARED SO THE APP IS LOADED ON WEBDAV REQUESTS. Without this the
    trash-purge hook is dead code, and it is invisible until you look for it.

    `register()` runs for every enabled app during bootstrap, but `boot()` only runs
    when an app is actually LOADED — and remote.php, the WebDAV entry point, loads a
    restricted set:

        $appManager->loadApps(['authentication']);
        $appManager->loadApps(['extended_authentication']);
        $appManager->loadApps(['filesystem', 'logging']);

    With no <types>, this app was never in that set, so boot() never ran on a DAV
    request. Everything wired in register() — the copy, move, rename, create,
    soft-delete and restore listeners — kept working, which is exactly what made the
    gap so hard to see: only the ONE thing wired in boot(), the legacy trash-purge
    hook ({@see OCA\GrafanaSync\Listener\TrashPurgeHook}), was silently dead. A
    perfectly correct hook, connected in a method that never ran.

    The cost here is specific: with the recycle bin ON, emptying the Nextcloud trash
    is the step that permanently deletes the parked dashboard from the Grafana bin
    folder. A dead purge hook means those dashboards accumulate in the bin forever —
    the admin empties their trash and Grafana quietly keeps every "deleted" dashboard.

    `filesystem` is the type for apps that must be present when the filesystem is in
    play, which is what a file-event-driven mirror is. Found in the n8n sibling first
    (saga §5.7), where the same hook was dead for the same reason.
  -->
  <types>
    <filesystem/>
  </types>
  <website>https://github.com/kubed-io/nextcloud-grafana</website>
  <bugs>https://github.com/kubed-io/nextcloud-grafana/issues</bugs>
  <repository type="git">https://github.com/kubed-io/nextcloud-grafana.git</repository>
  <dependencies>
    <!-- 31, not 30: AutoSyncSettings implements IDeclarativeSettingsFormWithHandlers
         (@since 31.0.0), which is the only way an ADMIN declarative form can carry a
         CHECKBOX at all — core's INTERNAL storage throws a TypeError on both the read
         and the write of a bool (see that class's docblock). This app has two, and one
         of them (bin_enabled) decides whether a delete is recoverable. Nextcloud 30
         reached end of life in 2025, so nothing supported is lost. -->
    <nextcloud min-version="32" max-version="34"/>
  </dependencies>
  <settings>
    <!-- Section order (by priority): Instance (5, base URL + token in one card —
         Grafana has one API + one credential, so no split) → Sync Settings (20,
         push timing + scheduled pull) → Folder mappings (30) → Sync Actions (45,
         all the buttons). Instance + Sync Settings are declarative forms registered
         in AppInfo\Application::register(). AdminTest is NOT rendered as its own
         panel — it stays only as the #[AuthorizedAdminSetting] target for the test
         endpoint; the Test-connection button lives in the Sync Actions panel. -->
    <admin>OCA\GrafanaSync\Settings\MappingSettings</admin>
    <admin>OCA\GrafanaSync\Settings\SyncSettings</admin>
    <admin-section>OCA\GrafanaSync\Settings\AdminSection</admin-section>
  </settings>
  <repair-steps>
    <!--
      Installs the application/grafana+json mimetype + icon mapping on every
      install/upgrade. Idempotent; see lib/Migration/RegisterMimetype.php.
    -->
    <install>
      <step>OCA\GrafanaSync\Migration\RegisterMimetype</step>
    </install>
    <post-migration>
      <step>OCA\GrafanaSync\Migration\RegisterMimetype</step>
      <!-- Deletes the retired grafana:sync/link/unmapped/ignore pills once. Not the
           `grafana` FOLDER tag, which is a different thing and stays. Idempotent;
           see lib/Migration/RemoveModePills.php. -->
      <step>OCA\GrafanaSync\Migration\RemoveModePills</step>
      <!-- Renames already-synced `*.grafana.json` files to `*.grafana`. Runs AFTER
           RegisterMimetype so the renamed files land on a mimetype that exists.
           Without it an upgraded instance's dashboards go unrecognised; see
           lib/Migration/MigrateFileExtension.php. -->
      <step>OCA\GrafanaSync\Migration\MigrateFileExtension</step>
    </post-migration>
    <uninstall>
      <!-- Mirror image of RegisterMimetype: reverts the core-tree mimetype/icon
           registration so removing the app leaves Nextcloud clean (store rule).
           Touches only the system registration — never user files/metadata/Grafana.
           See lib/Migration/UnregisterMimetype.php. -->
      <step>OCA\GrafanaSync\Migration\UnregisterMimetype</step>
    </uninstall>
  </repair-steps>
  <commands>
    <command>OCA\GrafanaSync\Command\SetToken</command>
    <command>OCA\GrafanaSync\Command\TestConnection</command>
    <command>OCA\GrafanaSync\Command\AddMapping</command>
    <command>OCA\GrafanaSync\Command\ListMappings</command>
    <command>OCA\GrafanaSync\Command\RemoveMapping</command>
    <command>OCA\GrafanaSync\Command\SetGroups</command>
    <command>OCA\GrafanaSync\Command\Reconcile</command>
  </commands>
</info>
